passages.text:
For more than a century, the keepers of a lighthouse on the north shore of Lake Superior wrote down the weather every few hours. {{1|Their logbooks, now stored in a state archive,}} record wind direction, wave height, and the color of the sky at dawn. To the keepers, these notes were routine. {{2|Similarly,}} to climate scientists today they are treasures.

Historians first noticed the logbooks' value in the 1990s, when a graduate student {{3|digitizing}} old shipping records found that the keepers' entries matched storm reports from passing freighters. {{4|Unlike a freighter,}} the logs offered something the ships could not: an unbroken record from a single place. A ship passes through a storm in a few hours, but a lighthouse {{5|stays where it is, and it does not move,}} season after season.

Turning handwritten notes into usable data {{6|has proven}} slow work. Each keeper had a personal shorthand. One wrote "NW fr" for a fresh northwest wind; another simply drew an arrow. Volunteers now transcribe the pages online, and each page {{7|is checked by at least two different people before its numbers are added to the database.}}

The effort is already paying off. Comparing the keepers' records with modern measurements, researchers {{8|have figured out}} that the lake now freezes, on average, about two weeks later than it did a century ago. {{9|The keepers, who never imagined their notes would be read this way, simply wanted to keep the light burning and the ships safe.}} {{10|Their careful habits have given scientists a rare gift: a long view of a changing lake.}}

When the Westbrook Public Library added a seed library in 2019, the librarians were not sure anyone would use it. The idea was simple: patrons could {{1|check up}} a packet of seeds in spring, grow the plants, and return seeds saved from the harvest in the fall. {{2|The library's first catalog listed forty varieties, most of them donated by a single retired farmer.}}

The first spring, only a dozen patrons borrowed seeds. {{3|Therefore,}} by the next year, word had spread through the town's garden club and its two elementary schools, and more than three hundred packets went out. Both {{4|schools science teachers}}, in particular, found that a packet of bean seeds could anchor a whole unit on plant life cycles. Students who planted the seeds at home {{5|bringing}} their harvests back to class in September.

Not every packet came back as hoped. Gardeners discovered that some vegetables, {{6|such as squash, cross-pollinate}} easily, so seeds saved from them may not grow true to the parent plant. {{7|Seeds from these plants can produce offspring that are different from the plants they came from.}} The librarians responded by labeling each packet "easy," "medium," or "advanced" to save, and the farmer's beans, which reliably breed true, became the most popular "easy" choice.

Today the seed library circulates more than a thousand packets a year. {{8|It's shelves}} hold heirloom tomatoes, drought-tolerant peppers, and {{9|a variety of corn which was grown by the founders of the town and which had nearly been lost entirely}}. {{10|No one at the library still wonders whether patrons will use the seeds.}}

For eleven years, the clock on the Harmon County Courthouse showed the same time: 4:17. Residents {{1|had long since stopped expecting it to move, they}} gave directions by it ("turn left when the clock says quarter past four") and joked that the county had finally found a way to stop time. {{2|The courthouse itself was built in 1902 from local limestone.}}

Ellison found the mechanism in worse shape than she {{3|expected. Pigeons}} had nested in the gear housing, and decades of grease had hardened into something closer to tar. {{4|Ellison, however, had spent thirty years repairing factory equipment, and she was not discouraged.}} She removed each gear, {{5|soaked it overnight in solvent, and scrubbing}} the teeth with a brass brush.

The hardest problem was a part worn beyond repair: a small toothed wheel, called the escapement wheel, {{6|that release}} the clock's stored energy in measured steps. {{7|Escapement wheels were first used in European clocks centuries ago.}} Ellison {{8|admired}} the worn edges of the broken original, calculated the angle of each tooth, and cut a new wheel by hand over the course of a winter.

On a Saturday morning in June, Ellison set the hands to the correct time and released the pendulum. The clock {{9|began to tick, and it was ticking}}. A small crowd had gathered on the courthouse lawn, and when the minute hand moved for the first time in eleven years, somebody started clapping. {{10|Within a week, a few residents admitted they no longer knew where to turn left.}}

Every morning at seven for fifty-one years, Harold Tibbs walked to the white wooden box in his backyard, {{1|opened its louvered door, and he read}} the two thermometers inside. He wrote the readings on a paper form, measured the rain in a narrow brass gauge, and mailed the forms to the National Weather Service at the end of each month. He was one of thousands of volunteer observers whose records form the backbone of the {{2|country's}} climate history.

{{3|Admittedly,}} Tibbs's records were not glamorous. They were, however, {{4|consistent, in}} fifty-one years he missed only nine mornings, each time arranging for a neighbor to take the readings. Scientists {{5|put up with}} this kind of consistency, because a climate record is only as useful as its longest unbroken stretch.

[1] When Tibbs began observing in 1952, his town had fewer than two thousand residents. [2] Today it has nearly twenty thousand, and the fields that once surrounded his yard have become parking lots and subdivisions. [3] Researchers studying how growing towns trap heat have used his records to measure the change. [4] {{6|They found that the town's summer nights warmed faster than the nearby countryside's, a pattern consistent with the spread of pavement.}}

Tibbs stopped observing in 2003, when his eyesight {{7|began failing him and getting worse}}. The box still {{8|stand}} in the yard, its paint peeling and its door no longer opened. {{9|But somewhere in a government archive, more than eighteen thousand of his mornings are kept, each one written down in pencil.}}

On the roof of a downtown hotel in Riverton, between the air-conditioning units and a row of potted herbs, sit four white wooden boxes. They hold roughly two hundred thousand honeybees, which the hotel's chef inspects every Tuesday morning. Urban hives often produce {{1|less}} honey than rural ones, but the chef is not trying to compete with farms. If the chef {{2|was}} to skip an inspection during the spring swarming season, half of a colony might leave for a nearby tree.

When the chef meets with the hotel's manager about the hives, {{3|he}} usually brings a jar of honey to the meeting. The hotel must also comply {{4|to}} the city's rules for beekeeping: hives must be registered, and inspectors visit twice a year. {{5|Even so,}} the number of rooftop hives in Riverton has tripled since 2015, and the city now counts dozens of beekeepers {{6|who's}} hives sit on roofs and balconies.

{{7|At the present time}}, the hotel sells its honey in the lobby. Guests often say that rooftop honey tastes different from {{8|the countryside}}, and they are right: city bees forage on linden trees, window boxes, and weedy lots, so each season's honey carries a slightly different flavor. {{9|The jars sell for twelve dollars each.}}

On a stretch of shingle beach near the English coast stand three strange concrete structures: a curved wall more than sixty meters long and two giant dishes {{1|facing, the sea}}. Built in the 1920s and 1930s, they {{2|was}} part of an experiment in hearing. Before radar existed, military planners hoped {{3|that sound could warn them of approaching aircraft}} before the planes came into view.

The idea was simple. A curved concrete surface gathers sound waves and concentrates them at a single point, {{4|where, a listener}} with a microphone could detect the drone of an engine miles away. In tests, the largest mirror could pick up aircraft roughly twenty miles off when conditions were ideal.

{{5|However,}} conditions were rarely ideal. Wind, waves, and the noise of nearby boats {{6|was drowning out}} faint engines, and as aircraft grew faster, the warning the mirrors provided shrank to just a few minutes. {{7|By the late 1930s, radar, which could detect planes far more reliably and at much greater distances, had made the mirrors obsolete.}}

Today the mirrors are protected {{8|landmarks, visitors}} can tour them on guided walks a few times a year. Standing in front of the largest one, a visitor who speaks quietly can be heard clearly by {{9|whomever}} stands at the focal point dozens of meters away. {{10|The site also has a small parking lot near the beach.}}

Every Friday after sunset, the parking lot behind the Ashford library becomes a market. By {{1|eight o'clock in the evening at night}}, more than forty vendors have set up folding tables, and the smell of grilled corn drifts across the street. The market began in 2016 with six stalls, {{2|most of them}} sold vegetables from nearby farms.

{{3|Not only did the market grow quickly, but it also changed}} the neighborhood around it. Storefronts on Elm Street, which had stood empty for years, {{4|now houses}} a bakery, a bike shop, and a tailor. Some longtime residents complain that the crowds make parking {{5|harder then}} it used to be. {{6|Others argue that the market has raised rents, pushing out some of the very families it was meant to serve.}}

The market's founder, a retired teacher named Rosa Ibarra, takes these concerns seriously. Last year she persuaded the city to reserve a third of the stalls for vendors who live within a mile of the market {{7|and waiving their fees}}. {{8|Walking through the market on a Friday night, the mix of languages is striking.}} Vendors call out prices in Spanish, Vietnamese, and Amharic, and {{9|whoever}} buys a mango from the stall near the entrance is likely to be handed a free sample of something else. {{10|Ibarra still works the information table herself most Fridays.}}

When the town of Alder Falls replaced its old streetlights in 2015, few residents {{1|expected, that}} the change would make news. The new fixtures, {{2|which pointed}} their light downward instead of outward, were chosen mainly to save electricity. Within a year, however, the town had become a destination for stargazers.

{{3|The town also hosts a popular summer fair each July.}} Under the old lights, which scattered their glow in every direction, only the brightest stars had been visible. Now, on clear nights, visitors spread blankets across the town park and watched meteors until dawn. The local diner began staying open past midnight, and two motels that had nearly closed were suddenly full on {{4|weekends; their}} owners credited the darkness.

Not everyone was pleased at first. A group of shop owners {{5|were}} worried that customers would feel unsafe on darker sidewalks. {{6|For example,}} the town council studied police records and found that reported crimes had not increased since the change. The council voted {{7|unanimously}} to keep the new lights and later passed a rule requiring that any new outdoor lights in town be shielded {{8|so that their light falls only downward toward the ground below them.}}

Today, Alder Falls holds a star party every August. Astronomers set up telescopes on the {{9|librarys'}} lawn, and children line up to see the rings of Saturn. {{10|The darkness that once worried shop owners is now part of the town's routine.}}

In 2014, a high school junior named {{1|Dana Ruiz, helped}} a local nonprofit obtain a license for a low-power FM radio station, which she then ran from her family's garage. The station's signal reached only about three miles, {{2|but}} within that circle lived nearly ten thousand people.

At first, the station played mostly music, much of it from Ruiz's own record collection, along with a weekly show of songs requested by classmates. Then listeners began calling in with requests that had nothing to do with songs: a lost dog, a church bake sale, a road closed by flooding. Ruiz, {{3|whom}} had planned to run a music station, found herself running a town bulletin board instead. {{4|For instance,}} within a year the station's schedule included a morning hour of local announcements, and that hour soon drew more listeners than {{5|any program}} on the station.

When a storm knocked out power across the county in 2016, the station, running on a {{6|generator, became}} the only source of news for many residents. For three {{7|consecutive days in a row}}, volunteers took turns at the microphone, reading updates on shelters, closed roads and places to find drinking water. After the storm, the county commissioners thanked the volunteers, and {{8|they}} voted to fund a permanent backup generator for the station.{{9|}}

Today the station is run by a board of volunteers, and Ruiz, now an engineer, still reads the announcements whenever she is home. The signal still reaches only about three miles, and the phone still rings with news of lost dogs and bake sales.{{10|}}

In April 1956, a converted oil tanker called the Ideal-X left Newark, New Jersey, bound for Houston, Texas. Its cargo was unremarkable: fifty-eight metal boxes {{1|that were full of ordinary, everyday, common goods}}. What made the voyage historic was the boxes themselves.

Before the 1950s, ships were loaded {{2|piece by piece. Dockworkers}} carried sacks, crates, and barrels aboard by hand or with small cranes, fitting each item into the hold like a piece of a puzzle. A ship could sit in port for a week or more while {{3|it's}} cargo was loaded and unloaded, and goods were often damaged or stolen along the way.

Malcom McLean, the trucking businessman {{4|whom organized}} the Ideal-X voyage, had spent years watching his trucks wait at crowded docks. His idea was simple: instead of unloading a truck's contents {{5|onto the dock. Why not lift}} the entire trailer body onto the ship? Containers of standard sizes could then move from truck to ship to train without ever being opened.

[1] The idea did not catch on overnight. [2] Ports had to build enormous new {{6|cranes and shipping}} companies had to agree on standard container sizes. [3] Dockworkers, many of whom feared for their jobs, resisted the change. [4] Within a few decades, however, containers had become the backbone of world trade. {{7|}}

Today, a single large container ship can carry the equivalent of more than twenty thousand twenty-foot containers, {{8|it}} can be unloaded in a few days. Shipping now accounts for only a small fraction of the price of most products. {{9|However,}} the toy, the shirt, or the phone on a store shelf may well have crossed an ocean in a box much like the ones aboard the Ideal-X. {{10|}}

On January 15, 1919, people in Boston's North End heard a sound like machine-gun fire. The rivets of a huge steel tank near the harbor were popping loose. The tank {{1|held: well over}} two million gallons of molasses, and within seconds all of it was pouring into the streets.

The molasses belonged to a company that turned it into industrial alcohol{{2|. Which was used}} to make explosives. The tank, about fifty feet tall, {{3|has leaked}} almost from the day it was first filled. Neighbors had collected the dripping molasses for their kitchens, and the company had simply painted the tank brown so that the leaks would be harder to see.{{4|}}

Because the day was unusually {{5|warm the}} molasses flowed more freely than it would have in colder weather. A wave of it, in places about twenty-five feet high, {{6|moved through the neighborhood}}. When the molasses reached the elevated railway, {{7|it buckled its steel supports}}. The flood killed twenty-one people, injured about 150 others, and {{8|it also flattened}} several buildings.

[1] The company claimed that someone had blown up the tank on purpose. [2] A court-appointed investigator then heard testimony from thousands of witnesses over several years. [3] In 1925, he found the company responsible {{9|for}} the disaster, concluding that the tank had been too weak to hold its load. [4] The case helped persuade Massachusetts to require that plans for such structures be checked and signed by a licensed engineer. {{10|}}

In the summer of 1858, a long spell of hot weather turned the River Thames into something close to an open sewer. Since 1800, London's population had {{1|grew}} to well over two million, and most of the city's waste flowed, by way of old drains and overflowing {{2|cesspool's,}} straight into the river. Newspapers soon gave the season a name: "the Great Stink."

Doctors at the time disagreed about what caused the city's repeated cholera {{3|outbreaks. Many}} blamed a poisonous "bad air" rising from filth, while a few argued that the disease spread through contaminated drinking water. Either way, the remedy pointed in the same direction: get the sewage out of the river.

Parliament, {{4|whose building stands beside the Thames,}} could hardly ignore the problem; lawmakers reportedly soaked the curtains in chemicals to block the smell. Within weeks, they passed a law giving the city's Metropolitan Board of Works the power and the money to act.

The board's chief engineer, Joseph Bazalgette, already had a plan. He proposed large intercepting sewers that would run alongside the river and catch waste before it reached the water. {{5|The sewers would carry the waste east. They would carry it to outfalls downstream of the city.}} Because much of the land was too flat for gravity alone to keep the flow moving, the system also needed {{6|four, large, pumping}} stations to lift the sewage. Bazalgette also planned for a city larger than the one he saw. {{7|}}

London's population today is more than three times {{8|as large as}} it was in 1858, and many of Bazalgette's tunnels still carry its waste. One of the original pumping stations, at Crossness, {{9|have}} been restored, and visitors can tour its ornate iron engine house. {{10|}}

Last summer I {{1|have spent}} six weeks on a folding chair at the corner of Fifth and Alder, counting cars. The city was deciding whether the intersection needed a traffic light, and it had hired a few students to gather the numbers.

My equipment was simple: a clipboard, a hand counter, and a sun hat. On a printed sheet, I recorded everything that passed, such {{2|as: cars,}} trucks, buses, and bicycles. Once, when a gust of wind threatened my papers, I {{3|lay}} the clipboard flat on the curb and held it down with my shoe.

The {{4|four hour}} shifts felt endless at first. After the first hour, {{5|you start}} to notice how slowly a single minute could pass.

Then I began to notice patterns. A red pickup turned left at 7:42 every morning, so reliably that I started checking my watch by it. {{6|}} Everyone {{7|accept}} the crossing guard, an older man named Mr. Abara, seemed to be in a hurry; he waved each child across as if he had all the time in the world. {{8|}}

By August, I could predict the corner's busy and quiet periods almost to the minute. The report{{9|, that the city published in October}} recommended a four-way stop instead of a light. I was a little disappointed, but I was not surprised. I had counted every car myself. {{10|}}

In the spring of 1860, a letter sent from Missouri to California by stagecoach usually took three weeks or more to arrive. A new company promised to deliver mail in about ten days, saving customers more than a {{1|weeks}} wait. Its service soon became known as the Pony Express.

The plan depended on speed at every step. The company set up a chain of relay stations across roughly 1,900 miles of prairie, mountains, and desert. Each rider, along with the mail he carried, {{2|were}} expected to cover about seventy-five to one hundred miles before handing off to a fresh rider. At each station along the way, he swapped his tired horse for a fresh {{3|one, and}} rode on. Station keepers had the next horse saddled and waiting, so each change took {{4|some time}}. Riders needed to be both brave and {{5|have a light weight}}, since every extra pound slowed the horses.

The mail itself traveled in a leather cover called a mochila, which fit over the saddle and could be moved from one horse to the next in seconds. Because of this system, the service was {{6|capable to}} remarkable feats. In March 1861, riders carried the text of Abraham Lincoln's first inaugural address between the ends of the telegraph lines in Nebraska and Nevada in under eight days. {{7|}} Despite such feats, the company was losing money. Postage was expensive, at first five dollars for every half ounce of mail, but the cost of the stations, horses, and riders was far greater. {{8|}}

In October 1861, workers completed the first transcontinental telegraph line, which could carry a message across the country almost instantly. Two days later, the Pony Express {{9|announces}} that it would close. {{10|}}

[1] Wilson Bentley grew up on a farm in Jericho, Vermont, where winters were long and snow fell for months at a time. [2] When he was fifteen, his mother gave him an old microscope, and he spent the next winter peering at snowflakes. [3] {{1|The crystals melted, however, before he could draw them, so he could never finish a sketch.}}

[4] Bentley decided that a camera might succeed where his pencil had failed. [5] He persuaded his parents to buy a bellows camera{{2|, which}} he attached to the microscope. [6] {{3|Working outdoors in an unheated shed, his breath had to be held}} so that its warmth would not melt the crystal on the slide. [7] He handled each flake with a feather {{4|and also moved it carefully with a thin wooden splint, both tools}} that would not melt the ice. [8] On January 15, 1885, after many failed attempts, he made the first known photograph of a single snow crystal.

[9] Over the next forty-six years, Bentley photographed more than five thousand snowflakes. [10] Some of {{5|his neighbors, who}} considered the project a waste of time, laughed at the farmer who stood in storms with a camera. [11] Scientists, {{6|however found}} his images valuable. [12] The photographs showed that each crystal's six arms grew in step with one another, a pattern that later researchers linked to the temperature and humidity the crystal passed through as it fell. {{7|}}

[13] In 1931, Bentley and the meteorologist William Humphreys published *Snow Crystals*, a book of more than two thousand of {{8|Bentley's}} images. [14] The collection of photographs that filled its pages {{9|were}} soon used by designers of textiles and jewelry as well as by scientists. [15] That December, Bentley walked six miles home through a blizzard, developed pneumonia, and died.

[16] {{10|Today, the shed where Bentley worked no longer stands, but the Jericho Historical Society keeps a collection of his glass-plate negatives.}} [17] Visitors can see the {{11|negatives which}} still preserve flakes that melted more than a century ago.

[1] In 1816, the French physician René Laennec faced an awkward problem. [2] A young patient had come to him with symptoms of heart disease, and the usual way to listen to a heart was for the doctor to press an ear directly against the patient's chest. [3] {{1|Laennec, who considered this method both unreliable and improper for this patient, looked for another way.}}

[4] He remembered seeing children play near a pile of wooden beams. [5] One child scratched the end of a beam with a pin, {{2|and the other child, listening at the far end, could hear}} the faint sound clearly. [6] Laennec rolled a sheet of paper into a tight tube, placed one end on the patient's chest, and put his ear to the other. [7] The heartbeat came through {{3|louder and clearer then}} it ever had with his ear alone.

[8] Over the next several years, Laennec {{4|experimented with tubes of different woods and lengths, and eventually settled}} on a hollow wooden cylinder about a foot long. [9] He called the instrument a *stethoscope*, from Greek words meaning "chest" and "to examine." [10] More important than the device {{5|itself, however was}} the careful method he developed for using it. [11] He listened to hundreds of patients, recorded the sounds he heard, and later compared {{6|them}} with what doctors found when they examined the patients' bodies after death.

[12] {{7|Laennec described his findings in a book published in 1819.}} [13] In it, he named and described sounds that doctors still learn to recognize, linking each to a particular disease of the heart or lungs. [14] Not every physician welcomed the {{8|change. Some}} complained that the stethoscope put a tube between the doctor and the patient. [15] Within a few decades, however, the instrument had become so common that {{9|they were}} a symbol of the medical profession itself.

[16] Today's stethoscopes, with two flexible tubes and a pair of earpieces, look little like Laennec's wooden cylinder. [17] {{10|Yet doctors who use them are still doing what he did in 1816: listening carefully to the body.}}

[1] In 1896, visitors to Coney Island in New York could ride a strange new attraction: a moving ramp that carried passengers up a gentle slope at an angle of about 25 degrees. [2] Its inventor, Jesse Reno, called it an "inclined elevator." [3] {{1|Riders stood on a belt of slats while a motor pulled the belt along, and the ride lasted only a few seconds.}}

[4] Within a few years, {{2|other inventors, were}} working on their own versions. [5] Charles Seeberger designed a moving stairway with flat steps that rose from a level platform, and he named it an "escalator," combining the Latin word for steps with the word "elevator." [6] The elevator company Otis bought Seeberger's patents and displayed its machine at the 1900 Paris Exposition, where it won a prize.

[7] Department stores were among the first buyers. [8] Store owners had noticed that shoppers rarely climbed stairs to reach upper floors {{3|and that the elevators, which held only a few people at a time, often had long lines.}} [9] An escalator could carry hundreds of people an hour and {{4|doesn't require}} an operator. [10] {{5|However,}} because riders could see the merchandise on each floor as they rose, the moving stairs encouraged them to browse.

[11] For decades, Otis treated "escalator" as a brand name and used it in {{6|their}} advertising. [12] Other companies had to call their machines "moving stairways." [13] In 1950, however, a federal office ruled that the word had become so common in everyday speech that it no longer identified a single company's product. [14] {{7|After the ruling, any manufacturer could use the word.}}

[15] Today, escalators carry millions of people each day through subway stations, airports, and shopping centers. [16] Few riders know that the word beneath their feet once {{8|belonged to}} a single company. [17] {{9|}}

[1] In 1950, several members of a Minneapolis chapter of Hadassah, a Jewish women's organization, brought a request to a local engineer named H. David Dalquist. [2] Some of them remembered a heavy, ring-shaped cake pan from their families' kitchens in Europe, {{1|and they wanted}} a lighter version that could be bought in American stores.

[3] {{2|Dalquist, whose small company,}} Nordic Ware, made kitchen products from cast aluminum, agreed to try. [4] The pan he designed had fluted sides and a tube in the center {{3|that let heat reach}} the middle of a dense batter. [5] He gave the pan a name, "Bundt," that his company could register as a trademark. {{5|}}

[6] For more than a decade, the pan sold {{4|poor}}. [7] {{6|One reason was that many}} shoppers had never heard of the ring-shaped cake it was meant to bake.

[8] That changed in 1966, when a home baker from Texas named Ella Helfrich entered a recipe in the Pillsbury Bake-Off, a national cooking contest. [9] {{7|Baked in a Bundt pan, a ring of soft fudge formed inside her "Tunnel of Fudge" cake.}} [10] The cake won second place, but it soon became {{8|the contests}} best-known entry. [11] The flood of requests from bakers who wanted to try the recipe {{9|were}} so large that Nordic Ware had to expand production to keep up.

[12] Today, the pan that Dalquist designed for a small group of club members is a fixture in kitchens across the country. [13] Its name, once known only in a few Minneapolis homes, now appears in cookbooks everywhere. {{10|}}

[1] In 1816, the Scottish scientist David Brewster was studying how light behaves when it reflects off glass. [2] While experimenting with two mirrors placed at an angle, he noticed that small objects between {{1|them was repeated}} in a symmetrical pattern. [3] Brewster built a tube around the mirrors, {{2|adding colored glass at one end, and}} called the device a kaleidoscope, from Greek words meaning "beautiful form" and "to look at."

[4] {{3|Brewster patented the device in 1817, however, his patent}} had a flaw. [5] A mistake in the paperwork left the design poorly protected, and copies appeared in shops {{4|almost immediately}}. [6] Within months, an enormous number of {{5|kaleidoscopes was sold}} in London and Paris. [7] Brewster{{6|, however}} earned little from them. {{7|}}

[8] Although he was disappointed, Brewster continued his research on light, which earned him many scientific honors. [9] Today he is remembered less for that research than for a toy {{8|that he hoped would also be useful to artists and designers}}.

[1] In 1943, Richard James, a mechanical engineer at a Philadelphia shipyard, was designing springs to keep delicate instruments steady on ships at {{1|sea. When}} one of his springs fell from a shelf. [2] Instead of landing in a heap, the spring stepped end over end across the floor, {{2|it's coils}} flipping one over another. [3] James spent the next two years testing different steels and coil sizes {{3|until he finds}} a spring that would walk reliably.

[4] His wife, Betty, searched the dictionary for a name and settled on "Slinky," a word meaning sleek and graceful. [5] In November 1945, the couple persuaded Gimbels, a Philadelphia department store, to let them demonstrate the {{4|toy; they}} worried, though, that no one would buy it. [6] They need not have worried: within ninety minutes, all four hundred of the Slinkys they had brought {{5|was sold}}. {{7|}} [7] {{6|Nevertheless,}} the toy, priced at one dollar, was being sold in stores across the country.

[8] Made from a single length of steel wire, {{8|children discovered that the toy could}} do far more than walk down stairs. [9] It could be stretched across a room, bounced like a yo-yo, or used by teachers to show how a wave travels. [10] {{9|Many other popular toys have also been invented by accident.}}

[1] In 1940, Frank Zamboni and two of his relatives opened Iceland, an ice rink in Paramount, California. [2] Smoothing the ice between sessions was slow work. [3] A team of workers had to scrape the surface, spray it with water, and squeegee it dry, a process that took more than an hour. [4] Skaters, many of {{1|who}} had paid for a full evening, spent that hour waiting.

[5] Zamboni, who had worked as an electrician and in refrigeration, decided to build a machine that could do the job {{2|alone. Spending}} most of the 1940s on the problem. [6] His first successful {{3|model which he finished in 1949 combined}} three {{4|tasks: shaving}} the ice, washing it, and leaving behind a thin, smooth layer of water. [7] {{5|The machine did the job faster.}}

[8] In 1950, the figure {{8|skater Sonja Henie}} saw the machine at Iceland and ordered two for her touring ice show. [9] Within a few years, rinks across the country were placing orders. [10] Each of the rinks that bought a machine {{6|were}} able to shorten the breaks between skating sessions. [11] Today, the name Zamboni is so familiar that many people use it for any ice resurfacer{{7|, regardless of who made it}}.

[1] After serving in the U.S. Navy during the Civil War, Lewis Latimer was hired at a Boston patent law firm as an office {{1|boy, his duties}} were mostly running errands. [2] Watching the firm's draftsmen prepare drawings for patent applications, {{2|Latimer taught himself the craft}} at night. [3] His skill eventually earned him a promotion to head draftsman. [4] In 1876, he prepared the drawings {{3|that}} accompanied Alexander Graham Bell's application for a telephone patent.

[5] Latimer then turned to electric lighting. [6] Early incandescent bulbs used carbon filaments that burned out quickly, and manufacturing them {{4|were}} slow and unreliable. [7] In 1882, Latimer patented an improved process for making carbon {{5|filaments, the process}} yielded filaments that were cheaper to produce. [8] {{6|However,}} he supervised the installation of electric lighting systems in several cities, including New York and Philadelphia.

[9] In 1884, Latimer joined Thomas Edison's company, where he {{7|worked and was employed}} as a draftsman and as an expert witness in patent cases. [10] He was later the only Black member of the Edison Pioneers, a group of {{8|Edisons}} earliest associates. [11] Latimer, who also wrote poetry and taught drafting to immigrants, {{9|died in 1928.}}
